   "type": "Feature",
   "properties": {
    "company": "Big Sandy and Cumberland Railroad",
    "line": "Big Sandy and Cumberland: Devon – Blackey",
    "id": "bsc-devon-blackey",
    "color": "#5C1A14",
    "mode": "intercity_rail",
    "avg_speed_kmh": 16,
    "speed_source": "Estimate only: a 3 ft 6 in narrow-gauge logging road worked by geared Shay/Climax engines with mixed trains. No timetable with running times has been found.",
    "geometry_source": "OpenStreetMap via Overpass (maps.mail.ru mirror), routed with opstrace.py 2026-10-27; Devon - Woodman hand-spliced from OSM road way 190045802 (KY 2059 Woodman Creek Road).",
    "geometry_quality": "16.5 mi drawn against 17.0 mi documented (17 I.C.C. 569 and SCC FY1905 '17.00'; SCC FY1910 '17.30'). No straight-line legs. Three parts. (1) Devon - Woodman, 2.4 mi, follows OSM road way 190045802 (KY 2059) round the Knox Creek meander from opposite Devon to Woodman. This is a road-following approximation of the narrow-gauge line, which left the N&W at Devon itself and followed the creek. The exact grade, and where it crossed the Tug Fork, are not in OSM. The drawn start is the Devon hamlet node, about 0.2 km across the Tug Fork from the road. (2) Woodman - Hurley, about 10.9 mi, is drawn on the present Norfolk Southern Buchanan Branch. OSM tags these ways note:old_railway_operator=BS&C. RULE 2 LATER-ALIGNMENT STATEMENT: this is the 1928-31 standard-gauge REBUILD of the BS&C, which the N&W operated from 1 July 1931, not the 1905-19 narrow-gauge track. The rebuild reused the Knox Creek trackbed (report: shared ~Lower Elk - Hurley), but curve easing, bridges and fills of the rebuild may differ locally from the narrow gauge, and no source maps the difference. The drawing does NOT use the 1930 Looney's Curve wye and 1,600-ft tunnel. The line passes 1.2 km from the Nominatim point for Kelsa (Paw Paw), which is a hamlet centre, not the station. (3) Hurley - Blackey, 3.2 mi drawn against 3.6 mi documented (ICC: Hurley 13.4, Blackey 17.0), is on OSM way 168899678. That is railway=abandoned, note:old_railway_operator=BS&C, the old grade abandoned in 1931. Its OSM fixme calls it a 'rough alignment', so treat its curvature as approximate. Blackey end is the grade vertex nearest the Blackey hamlet node, 0.3 km from it.",
    "segments": [
     {
      "start": 1905,
      "end": 1919,
      "headway_min": 1100,
      "note": "Mixed trains (no passenger-only trains are reported) over the 3 ft 6 in narrow gauge: Devon, W.Va. (N&W main line) - Lower Elk - Hurricane - Middle Elk (Bill) - Paw Paw (Kelsa) - Guesses Fork - Hurley - Blackey, Va. 1905 and 1919 are the FIRST and LAST CONFIRMED passenger years, NOT the opening and closing. 1905: the Virginia SCC return for the year to 30 June 1905 shows 11,920 revenue passengers, $2,651 passenger revenue and 15,968 mixed-train miles over 17 mi Devon - Blackey. The line was built from about 1900, so service probably began earlier, but no earlier year is proven. Other confirmed years: FY1906 (SCC), 1908 (passenger earnings $4,493.31, quoted in 17 I.C.C. 569) and 1911 (Fowler, company timetable). 1919: the SCC return for calendar 1919 shows BS&C passenger revenue and revenue passengers. Nothing has been found for 1920-1931. The BS&C ran its own trains until the N&W took over the rebuilt line on 1 July 1931, so 1931 bounds the end from above but does not date the last passenger. This is a withheld unknown: do not extend the segment either way without evidence. The N&W bought the BS&C stock on 12 Oct 1923, but the BS&C kept operating, so there is no ownership split inside the span. headway_min 1100 is an ESTIMATE: about 1.3 mixed round trips a day, derived from the FY1905 train miles, not read from a timetable. SOURCE CONFLICT: the Big Sandy News (15 Oct 1909) says the road had 'only been used for hauling lumber' and that passenger service would come with the extension past Blackey. The sworn 1905, 1906 and 1908 passenger revenue figures are followed instead. The paper may mean a dedicated passenger train as opposed to mixed trains.",
      "sources": [
       "Segment-level: start 1905 from the Virginia SCC annual report for FY ending 30 June 1905 (BS&C return: revenue passengers, passenger revenue, mixed-train miles, Devon - Blackey 17 mi). Also checked: FY1906 SCC passenger revenue; 1908 passenger earnings in 17 I.C.C. 569 (decided 8 Feb 1910). End 1919 from the SCC report for calendar 1919 (Tables 6 and 8, BS&C passenger revenue and revenue passengers; OCR columns mangled). Headway derived from FY1905 train miles (estimate). Conflicting Big Sandy News 15 Oct 1909 recorded in the note.",
       "https://archive.org/details/annualreportsta04commgoog",
